{"groups":[{"members":["c5","c2","c3","c4"],"primary":"c5","title":"California FEHA housing screening limits: source of income (incl. Section 8), aggregate income, credit history for subsidized applicants, and criminal history","requirement":"Housing providers may not refuse to rent based on lawful, verifiable source of income, including Section 8 and HUD-VASH vouchers, and income standards must count the aggregate income of people living together. Where a government rent subsidy exists, owners must base income standards on the tenant's portion of rent and, if they use credit history, must let the applicant provide alternative evidence of ability to pay; criminal-history blanket bans are prohibited, and arrests without conviction, sealed or expunged records, and juvenile records may not be considered.","key_value":"Source of income (incl. Section 8 and HUD-VASH vouchers) is protected; alternative evidence to credit history required where a government rent subsidy exists; no blanket criminal-history bans","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 12955","coverage_conditions":"Applies to most housing providers and housing-related services, including owners, landlords, tenant screening companies and property managers. Covered housing includes single-family homes, multi-family homes, condominiums, apartments, mobile homes and subsidized housing; no building-size or age limits stated. The credit-history and income-standard provisions apply where there is a government rent subsidy.","exemptions":"Owners may ask about the level or source of income, request employment verification and landlord references, and verify identity. An owner who lives in the home and rents to one additional person may exclude applicants based on protected characteristics, but may not use discriminatory advertising; senior housing may be limited by age. The sources do not say how these exceptions apply to source-of-income or criminal-history screening.","coverage_from":"c4","conflict_note":"Effective dates differ across members: D015 (c2) gives 2020-01-01 for the SB 329 source-of-income/Section 8 provision, and D027 (c5) gives 2024-01-01, which matches the later SB 267 credit-history amendment. Both appear to be accurate dates for different amendments to § 12955. 2024-01-01 is kept as the date of the current operative text."},{"members":["c1"],"primary":"c1","title":"California Civil Rights Council regulations on criminal history in housing decisions","requirement":"Regulations limit what criminal history a housing provider may seek and how it may be considered. Blanket bans on applicants with a criminal record are prohibited, as are policies that consider arrests without convictions, but landlords are not entirely barred from checking criminal history.","key_value":"Blanket bans on applicants with a criminal record prohibited; arrests without convictions may not be considered","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2020-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"California Civil Rights Council (formerly Fair Employment and Housing Council) regulations on use of criminal history in housing","coverage_conditions":"Housing decisions generally; no building-level coverage limits stated","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
